C = [
("h1", "2. Where Paul Commands and Where He Leaves It Open"),
("p", "Claim: Paul himself marks the difference between what he commands and what he leaves to the believer. The passages on liberty depend on that difference, and the volume reads them in the light of it."),
("sub", "2.1 Paul’s own markers"),
("p", "Paul is careful to say when he speaks by commandment and when he does not. In 1 Corinthians 7 he says four things in the space of twenty verses. “But I speak this by permission, and not of commandment” (1 Corinthians 7:6). “And unto the married I command, yet not I, but the Lord” (1 Corinthians 7:10). “But to the rest speak I, not the Lord” (1 Corinthians 7:12). “Now concerning virgins I have no commandment of the Lord: yet I give my judgment” (1 Corinthians 7:25). The chapter is not taken up here, because its subject is marriage and singleness, which the volume leaves to a separate study. The markers matter for this volume for another reason. They show that Paul does not treat every sentence he writes as the same kind of sentence, and that he tells the reader which kind it is."),
("p", "The same care appears in 1 Corinthians 14:37, where Paul says that what he writes “are the commandments of the Lord,” and in 1 Corinthians 7:40, where he gives his judgment and adds, “and I think also that I have the Spirit of God.” The volume takes this as the standard for reading the passages on liberty. Where Paul says “flee,” “put away” or “shall not inherit,” he commands. Where he says “let every man be fully persuaded in his own mind” (Romans 14:5), he does not. The reader who treats the second kind as the first has changed what Paul wrote."),
("sub", "2.2 What he commands"),
("p", "Some conduct is not left open. “Flee fornication” (1 Corinthians 6:18). “Wherefore, my dearly beloved, flee from idolatry” (1 Corinthians 10:14). Of the man who is called a brother and is a fornicator, covetous, an idolater, a railer, a drunkard or an extortioner, Paul writes, “with such an one no not to eat” (1 Corinthians 5:11). He lists those who “shall not inherit the kingdom of God” (1 Corinthians 6:9, 10), and he names the works of the flesh with the same warning: “they which do such things shall not inherit the kingdom of God” (Galatians 5:21). The volume reports the warnings as written. It does not decide whose kingdom is meant or what the warning means for a believer who falls, because the passages do not say, and it reads the lists as what they are: matters on which Paul gives no liberty."),
("sub", "2.3 What he leaves open"),
("p", "Other conduct he leaves to the believer. In Rome one believer “believeth that he may eat all things: another, who is weak, eateth herbs” (Romans 14:2). “One man esteemeth one day above another: another esteemeth every day alike” (Romans 14:5). In Corinth, meat sold in the market may be eaten, “asking no question for conscience sake” (1 Corinthians 10:25). Paul does not command eating in Rome or abstaining in Corinth. He says of meat, “there is nothing unclean of itself” (Romans 14:14), and, “But meat commendeth us not to God: for neither, if we eat, are we the better; neither, if we eat not, are we the worse” (1 Corinthians 8:8)."),
("ctbl", "What Paul commands and what he leaves open.", [
    ("Matter", "Commanded, or left open", "Where"),
    ("Fornication", "Commanded: flee", "1 Corinthians 6:18"),
    ("Idolatry", "Commanded: flee", "1 Corinthians 10:14"),
    ("The works of the flesh", "Named with a warning; no liberty", "Galatians 5:19–21"),
    ("A brother who is a fornicator, covetous, an idolater, a railer, a drunkard or an extortioner", "Commanded: not to keep company, not to eat", "1 Corinthians 5:11"),
    ("Eating all things or herbs", "Left open: let neither despise nor judge", "Romans 14:2, 3"),
    ("Esteeming one day above another", "Left open: fully persuaded in his own mind", "Romans 14:5"),
    ("Meat sold in the shambles", "Left open: eat, asking no question", "1 Corinthians 10:25"),
    ("Meat said to be offered in sacrifice, when someone says so", "Left open, with a limit: eat not for his sake that shewed it", "1 Corinthians 10:28"),
    ("Meat in the idol’s temple", "A limit is given: it may embolden the weak", "1 Corinthians 8:10"),
  ], [0.36, 0.40, 0.24]),
("sub", "2.4 Why the line matters"),
("p", "A believer who treats an open matter as commanded will judge his brother, and Paul forbids it: “Who art thou that judgest another man’s servant?” (Romans 14:4). A believer who treats a commanded matter as open will fall into the flesh, and Paul forbids that too: “Be not deceived” (1 Corinthians 6:9). The two errors run in opposite directions, and Paul warns against both in the same letters. The Galatians were being asked to treat circumcision as commanded, and Paul wrote, “Stand fast therefore in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free” (Galatians 5:1). The Corinthians were treating fornication as a matter of liberty, and Paul wrote, “All things are lawful unto me, but all things are not expedient” (1 Corinthians 6:12) and then, “Flee fornication.”"),
("sub", "2.5 What the volume takes from the line"),
("bul", [
    "Paul marks, in his own words, the difference between a commandment and his judgment or permission.",
    "Some conduct is commanded or forbidden, and no liberty is given there.",
    "Some conduct is left to the believer, and Paul gives limits there, not a command.",
    "To confuse the two is an error in either direction, and Paul warns against both.",
  ]),
]
